  "body": "On the pinned PR799 X4 compensation panel, two paid target shears followed by joint restoration reduce the strongest pair from 648 to 356 primitives per real component. The best same-pair zero/one-shear choice costs 484, so the new mechanism saves 256 real primitives beyond that baseline. This is a finite complex-supplier research result; no new kappa or full supplier admission is claimed.\n\nThe complete 447,552-case census finds 1,456 improvements on 396 pairs. A separate Fraction-matrix implementation with explicitly emitted binary-Horner primitives reproduces the full histogram and every pair's cost floors. The winning forward and inverse-transpose programs match on 219 independent target/helper/dirty-work columns, reject 8 mathematical mutations, and pass 8 source/receipt integrity controls. Original source pins, selected snapshot and attribution are retained.\n\nLocal cancellation-free bounds grow 24->57 forward and 2->5 inverse-transpose. Whole-program precision, native frame/phase replay, global invoices, cold supplier/full repository suites, hosted CI and Lean remain unexecuted. No compiler or dependency was installed.\n\nReproduce with standard-library Python:\n\n```sh\npython -I -B research/x4-two-shear-seed31/verify.py\npython -I -B research/x4-two-shear-seed31/crosscheck.py\npython -I -B research/x4-two-shear-seed31/test_package.py\n```\n\nCredits: ToikY PR901/909 and Sricharan Vagvala PR749 for restored-target methods; Jeremy Kothe PR799, Chafik Boukhalfa PR741 and icekylinx's construction/emitter lineage. PR937/947's center-factor shears concern a different matrix and boundary. Prepared for @woklerx with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance; Apache-2.0. Both implementations are AI-written, not independent human review.\n",
